    "description": "Iran experiences destructive flooding despite recurrent drought, as intense rainfall can rapidly overwhelm rivers and settlements. The floods of 2019 demonstrate how atmospheric moisture, mountain terrain, infrastructure damage, and disrupted health services can combine in a prolonged disaster. [s1][s3][s4]",
    "tags": ["Floods", "Extreme rainfall", "Water", "Zagros Mountains", "Disaster recovery"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T12:35:27Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T12:35:27Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Floods in Iran: Rainfall, Rivers and Recovery | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iran’s flood hazards through the 2019 disaster, mountain rainfall processes, damage to essential services, and the limits of recovery evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "rainfall-and-terrain",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How extreme rain becomes a regional disaster",
        "content": "Iran’s flood history challenges the assumption that a drought-prone country has little reason to prepare for excess water. Research on the March 2019 floods identified an atmospheric river: a concentrated corridor transporting water vapour through the atmosphere. As this moisture crossed the Zagros Mountains, the terrain forced air upward, helping produce exceptionally heavy precipitation. This is called orographic forcing. Mountains therefore influenced where the rain fell, while the weather system supplied moisture from far beyond Iran. [s1]\n\nThe concentration of rainfall also mattered. The University of California, Irvine’s Center for Hydrometeorology and Remote Sensing documented successive extreme events affecting northern and western Iran. Its satellite analysis showed that a small number of very wet days contributed a substantial share of the rainfall accumulated during the disaster period. An annual rainfall total alone would conceal this concentration and provide an incomplete explanation of the flooding. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "disrupted-services",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Damage continues after the water arrives",
        "content": "The World Health Organization’s retrospective account describes flooding beginning in Mazandaran and Golestan in March 2019, followed by additional waves elsewhere. Its response concentrated particularly on Lorestan and Golestan. These locations illustrate the geographic spread of the emergency rather than a single flooded river basin. [s3]\n\nFlood damage extended beyond inundated homes. WHO documented damaged health facilities, obstructed roads, landslides, and standing water inside buildings. These conditions limited access both to affected communities and to medical services. Delivering supplies was only one part of the response: maintaining electricity, assessing facility damage, and strengthening disease surveillance were also necessary. The account shows why a flood’s consequences cannot be measured adequately by its visible water extent or immediate casualties alone. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "recovery-and-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Understanding recovery without overstating it",
        "content": "Following the disaster, Iran’s Plan and Budget Organization commissioned a government-led assessment with the United Nations. It examined Golestan, Lorestan, and Khuzestan, drawing on field surveys, ministry information, and sector specialists. Its scope included housing, agriculture, transport, water and sanitation, livelihoods, and environmental issues. This approach treated recovery as the restoration of connected systems rather than the replacement of individual damaged structures. [s4]\n\nThe assessment’s geographic boundary matters. Findings for selected provinces cannot automatically describe every affected locality, and an estimate of recovery needs does not prove that reconstruction subsequently occurred. The UN publication page establishes how the assessment was organized; it does not independently verify later implementation. [s4]\n\nFor readers comparing Iranian flood events, the most useful questions are therefore specific: where did the rain fall, over what interval, which services failed, and what evidence documents recovery? The 2019 sources answer different parts of that sequence. Together they explain the disaster more clearly than a single national damage figure. [s1][s2][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "mountain-rainfall-process",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "A documented rainfall mechanism in March 2019",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Atmospheric moisture transport\"] -->|\"Supplied moisture to\"| B[\"Weather system over the region\"]\n  B -->|\"Carried moist air toward\"| C[\"Zagros Mountains\"]\n  C -->|\"Forced air upward\"| D[\"Orographic precipitation\"]\n  D -->|\"Contributed to\"| E[\"March 2019 flooding\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows describe the atmospheric and terrain mechanism identified for the March 2019 event. They do not attribute every flood in Iran to the same mechanism or explain all local damage.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      }
    ],
